    "q-66666": {
      "dom_id": "step-q66666",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-3,$ then what is $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-6\\pi )+4?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-3$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-6\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-3,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-6\\pi )=-3.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-6\\pi )+4 & =3(-3)+4 \\\\  & =-9+4 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
